# AMD64 thunk export spec -- vcruntime140.dll.
#
# Added from the 2026-08-15 first-real-game triage: with the application's own
# shipped DLLs now loadable (find_dll_file() searches the load path for the
# guest's machine instead of refusing it outright), what stopped
# quake2ex_steam.exe was Wine builtins with no guest thunk -- each reported as
# c000007b, the loader correctly refusing to splice a native ppc64 module into
# a guest call.  Nothing here is hand-written: the export list comes from this
# module's own .spec.
DLL vcruntime140.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-08-17 DOOM triage: hosting MICROSOFT'S OWN vcruntime CLIENTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DOOM (2016) loads Razer's CChromaEditorLibrary.dll, which pulls in
# Microsoft's real mfc140u.dll -- a NATIVE x86-64 PE, so a guest image, so
# every one of its imports has to be served from this machine's thunk surface.
# Six of the VCRUNTIME140 names it imports were refused here as "no declaration
# found in Wine headers" and became ordinal HOLES; the guest loader bound
# ntdll's sentinel to each, and mfc140u's DllMain called the first of them --
# __vcrt_InitializeCriticalSectionEx -- straight into 0xDEAD0001.
#
# The gap was tooling, not ABI.  Wine implements all six; it just declares them
# in private headers or in no header at all.  So:
#
#   * _purecall comes from dlls/msvcrt/msvcrt.h via PROBE-EXTRA, exactly the
#     way ucrtbase.thunks already gets _lock/_unlock/_amsg_exit.  That is a
#     real clang-checked declaration, not an assertion.
#
#   * the other three use the .spec location column, the documented downgrade
#     for an export Wine declares in NO header.  A .spec pins the arity and the
#     argument classes but carries no return type, so the <int|void> column
#     below is an unchecked human assertion.  Each is justified against the
#     implementation it forwards to, named here so the claim can be rechecked:
#       __std_terminate               forwards to ucrtbase.terminate (see
#                                     ucrtbase.spec:147), which is noreturn.
#       __std_type_info_destroy_list  dlls/msvcrt/cpp.c:1071 --
#                                     void CDECL (SLIST_HEADER *).
#       __vcrt_InitializeCriticalSectionEx
#                                     dlls/vcruntime140/misc.c:46 --
#                                     BOOL CDECL (CRITICAL_SECTION*, DWORD,
#                                     DWORD).  BOOL is int class.
#
# __CxxFrameHandler3/4 ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT HOLES, and must stay one.  They
# are not an API the guest calls; each is the LANGUAGE PERSONALITY ROUTINE
# named by the guest image's own .pdata/UNWIND_INFO, invoked BY the unwinder
# with that image's exception and context records and expected to walk that
# image's funclet tables and resume in its code.  Thunking either would send
# a guest's unwind into the host's ppc64 __CxxFrameHandler, which has neither
# the guest's frame layout nor any way to resume x86-64 execution -- a silent
# wrong answer where the hole is a named, diagnosable sentinel.  Serving them
# needs a real guest-side EH personality, not an export line -- guest-cxx-
# eh-plan.md's Session B/C, not done here.  Nothing on mfc140u's LOAD path
# reaches __CxxFrameHandler3 (measured: the module now initialises and its
# exports call), and a guest that actually throws C++ across this boundary
# must fail loudly.
#
# WHAT THIS COMMENT COULD NOT SAY ON 2026-08-17: a THIRD vehicle exists now.
# _CxxThrowException used to sit in this same "no vehicle exists yet" bucket
# and was trap-thunked below (0x00000B01) as the least-bad option at the
# time.  It no longer is one: dlls/guestcrt + spec2thunk FORWARD (landed
# 2026-08-22 for setjmp/longjmp, commit b9553bf3403) means a register- or
# stack-contract export can be REAL x86-64 code in a guestpe-built module,
# reached by an ordinary PE forwarder the guest loader follows itself -- see
# the 2026-08-29 triage below.  __CxxFrameHandler3/4 stay refused not because
# no vehicle exists, but because nobody has written the guest-side FH3/FH4
# personality that would go behind it; the day one exists, it forwards here
# exactly the way _CxxThrowException now does.
INCLUDE-DIR   ../msvcrt
PROBE-EXTRA   msvcrt.h        # _purecall

__std_terminate                    0x00000B02  0  void  vcruntime140.spec:42
__std_type_info_destroy_list       0x00000B03  1  void  vcruntime140.spec:44
__vcrt_InitializeCriticalSectionEx 0x00000B04  3  int   vcruntime140.spec:55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-08-22 Quake II triage (S4): setjmp/longjmp -- forwards, like
# Wine's own .spec
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# quake2ex_steam.exe statically imports __intrinsic_setjmp and longjmp from
# THIS module and died on sentinel 0xDEAD0007 the moment it called the
# first.  Both are register-contract exports no trap stub can carry (the
# stub would save native ppc64 registers, not the guest's -- see
# dlls/guestcrt/setjmp.c), so this thunk forwards them exactly where
# vcruntime140.spec:35/75 already forwards them: ucrtbase, whose own thunk
# forwards on to the real x86-64 code in guestcrt.dll.  Keeping the
# spec's own hop (rather than jumping straight to guestcrt) means every
# CRT-family thunk that grows these names forwards to ONE place, and the
# chain is the same one a prefix-staged Proton vcruntime140 takes -- so
# both mixes resolve identically.
#
# __intrinsic_setjmpex stays a deliberate hole for the reason named in
# ucrtbase.thunks: it is the unwinding variant, and guestcrt's longjmp
# never unwinds.
FORWARD __intrinsic_setjmp ucrtbase.__intrinsic_setjmp
FORWARD longjmp            ucrtbase.longjmp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The 2026-08-29 guest-cxx-eh-plan Session A triage: the throw half, and a
# mis-served language handler
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# game_x64.dll (Quake II's 2023 remaster) and libxess*.dll (Cyberpunk's XeSS)
# both statically import VCRUNTIME140._CxxThrowException and
# VCRUNTIME140.__C_specific_handler; neither imports __CxxFrameHandler3, and
# both import VCRUNTIME140_1.__CxxFrameHandler4 (see guest-cxx-eh-plan.md,
# section 3, for the measured import tables).  Two fixes:
#
#   * _CxxThrowException moves from the 0x00000B01 trap line above (deleted)
#     to a FORWARD, following vcruntime140.spec:2's own hop to
#     ucrtbase._CxxThrowException.  ucrtbase's thunk carries the second hop,
#     on to the real x86-64 code in dlls/guestcrt/cxxthrow.c -- see that
#     file's banner for why a trap can never be right here: it would raise
#     the exception over the HOST's native stack, and the dispatcher's search
#     phase would then walk frames with no .xdata at all and never reach a
#     guest __CxxFrameHandler3/4, however it is eventually served.  This was
#     the DEAD0009 sentinel measured on this triage's own titles.  (An
#     earlier draft of this comment attributed part of that sentinel to a
#     prefix-staged Proton vcruntime140's own forward "dying at load" --
#     WRONG, corrected same-day by adversarial review: dlls/ntdll/loader.c's
#     guest module resolution tries the BUILTIN thunk before the ordinary
#     search path a prefix-staged file would be found on
#     (find_dll_file's machine_dir branch, ~3540-3565), so for an unqualified
#     import a same-named builtin always wins and a staged file is never even
#     opened.  DEAD0009 was purely the builtin-lane bug: this module's own
#     old trap line, full stop, in every prefix.  There was one bug and one
#     symptom, not two.)
#
#   * __C_specific_handler was, until this line, whatever FROM-SPEC auto's
#     default machinery produces for a name winnt.h declares: an ordinary
#     TRAP stub.  That is wrong for exactly one reason, but it is a serious
#     one -- dlls/ntdll/signal_ppc64.c's frame walk recognises __C_specific_
#     handler by ADDRESS IDENTITY (guest_c_specific_handler_addr(), matched
#     against the guest ntdll's own export) so it can serve the call with a
#     real, guest-frame-aware implementation instead of entering it as
#     unknown guest code.  A trap stub here is a SEPARATE address that fails
#     that identity check; the frame would then be entered "as guest code",
#     immediately execute the stub's `mov r10,rcx; syscall`, and trap into
#     the native ppc64 __C_specific_handler with an AMD64 DISPATCHER_CONTEXT
#     it cannot interpret -- a silent wrong answer for the first /MD __try
#     frame dispatch crosses (INFERRED consequence in guest-cxx-eh-plan.md
#     section 1, not yet observed in a log; probes/check-cxx-throw.sh's
#     compiled __try lane is what actually exercises this).  The fix is the
#     same shape as setjmp/longjmp and _CxxThrowException above: forward
#     along the spec's own hop (vcruntime140.spec:13 already names
#     ucrtbase.__C_specific_handler) so the guest IAT ends up holding
#     ucrtbase's thunk address, which itself forwards on to ntdll's -- the
#     one address identity recognises.
FORWARD _CxxThrowException   ucrtbase._CxxThrowException
FORWARD __C_specific_handler ucrtbase.__C_specific_handler

# CORRECTION, 2026-08-29 (same day, adversarial review): this module's .spec
# also declares __CxxRegisterExceptionObject/__CxxUnregisterExceptionObject/
# __DestructExceptionObject (vcruntime140.spec:22-24, each a spec-level
# forward to the ucrtbase namesake -- which parse_wine_spec does not turn
# into a thunk-level FORWARD, so each still needed its own signature), and
# msvcrt.h:156-158 declares all three.  PROBE-EXTRA msvcrt.h (above, for
# _purecall) is enough for the oracle to see them, so FROM-SPEC auto emitted
# all three as ordinary native trap stubs here too -- MEASURED at RVA
# 0x2030/0x2040/0x2050 in the built vcruntime140.dll, alongside the identical
# landmine in ucrtbase (see that file's matching correction for why a trap is
# wrong: __DestructExceptionObject calls the thrown object's own destructor,
# a GUEST function pointer, so a native trap means native ppc64 code
# indirect-calling x86-64 the moment any FH3/FH4 personality reaches it).
# Nothing in this file ever claimed these three "stay sentinels" -- that
# claim was made in ucrtbase.thunks and dlls/guestcrt/cxxthrow.c's banner --
# but this module carried the same live trap regardless, undocumented.
# EXCLUDE below makes it a real, diagnosable refusal.
EXCLUDE __CxxRegisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __CxxUnregisterExceptionObject
EXCLUDE __DestructExceptionObject
